[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Jakubovitz, and Giryes, 2018-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba6bc847a6ece8448ff492d0639a93d736427903)
- venue: European Conference on Computer Vision
- citation count: 253
[method]
The paper evaluates Jacobian regularization as a smoothness-control defense for neural classifiers and compares it with Cross-Lipschitz regularization, Input Gradient regularization, and adversarial training. The Jacobian regularization method adds a penalty on the Frobenius norm of the input-output Jacobian during a post-processing training phase after standard training has already achieved high clean accuracy. This is related to the proposed research method because it uses a norm-based smoothness penalty to reduce model sensitivity to input perturbations, which is conceptually aligned with controlling a Lipschitz constant to bound worst-case loss under perturbed covariates in robust CATE estimation. The experiments are conducted on MNIST, CIFAR-10, and CIFAR-100 image classification tasks. Robustness is evaluated under three attacks: DeepFool, which targets the l2 metric and is measured by the average proportion between the minimal l2 perturbation norm and the input l2 norm; FGSM, which targets the linf metric and is measured by test accuracy on perturbed inputs; and JSMA, which targets the l0 metric and is also measured by test accuracy on perturbed inputs.
[results]
Under DeepFool, Jacobian regularization produced substantially smaller perturbation norms required to fool the network than adversarial training, Input Gradient regularization, and Cross-Lipschitz regularization. Combining Jacobian regularization with adversarial training further increased this advantage. Clean test accuracy changes were small: on MNIST, Jacobian regularization, Cross-Lipschitz regularization, and adversarial training caused a small accuracy decrease, while Input Gradient regularization improved accuracy; on CIFAR-10, Jacobian regularization, Cross-Lipschitz regularization, and adversarial training improved accuracy, while Input Gradient regularization reduced accuracy. Under FGSM, Jacobian regularization achieved higher perturbed-test accuracy than Input Gradient regularization, Cross-Lipschitz regularization, and adversarial training alone, and the advantage became more distinct when combined with adversarial training. The minimal original test accuracy using Jacobian regularization was 98% on MNIST and 88.49% on CIFAR-10. Under JSMA, Jacobian regularization was superior on CIFAR-10, inferior to Input Gradient regularization on MNIST, but better than Cross-Lipschitz regularization, and was described as competitive overall. The paper attributes the weaker MNIST JSMA result to a mismatch between the l2-oriented Jacobian penalty and the l0-style single-pixel perturbation used by JSMA.
[context]
The experiments used post-processing Jacobian regularization rather than applying the regularization from the beginning of training, because starting with the regularization from the beginning yielded lower final test accuracy. For MNIST, the network had two convolutional layers each followed by max pooling, then two fully connected layers, ReLU activations, softmax output, dropout with 0.5 keep probability, Adam optimization, and a mini-batch size of 500; it achieved 99.08% clean test accuracy, and Jacobian regularization used lambda = 0.1. For CIFAR-10, the network had four sets of two convolutional layers followed by max pooling and dropout with 0.75 keep probability, then two fully connected layers, RMSProp optimization, and a mini-batch size of 128; it achieved 88.79% clean test accuracy, and Jacobian regularization used lambda = 0.5. CIFAR-100 results were reported as generally consistent with MNIST and CIFAR-10. Ablations over the regularization weight lambda were provided in the appendix, and adversarial training results used the amount of perturbed inputs in the training mini-batch that gave the best robustness in each test case. Hardware details and exact runtime were not specified in the provided text.
[end related work 4]

