[method]
The paper studies adversarial training with an explicit robust regularization term, using TRADES and standard adversarial training on WideResNet-34 classifiers. It varies the robust regularization parameter lambda over 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, and 21 for TRADES, and over 1.00, 1.25, 1.50, 1.75, and 2.00 for standard adversarial training, while changing network width using widen factors 1, 5, and 10. The method defines perturbation stability as the fraction of examples whose prediction remains unchanged within an Lp perturbation ball, and uses this metric to diagnose how strongly the robust regularization term controls prediction stability. It also links perturbation stability to local Lipschitzness and studies how network width affects this relationship. The same regularization-strength tuning strategy is extended to CIFAR100 and DenseNet-BC-40 with different growth rates, with evaluation under PGD, C&W, FAB, and Square attacks. This relates to the proposed research method because the paper’s lambda-controlled adversarial objective and perturbation-stability analysis are an empirical analogue of bounding adversarial loss through factual loss plus a Lipschitz or smoothness term, supporting the research plan’s use of Lipschitz regularization for robustness under covariate perturbation.
[results]
Perturbation stability decreases monotonically as network width increases, while robust accuracy does not always improve with width. Increasing lambda consistently increases perturbation stability and shifts the best robust accuracy to larger lambda values. On CIFAR10 with TRADES, the best robust accuracy is 48.01% for width-1 at lambda = 9, 55.61% for width-5 at lambda = 12, and 56.43% for width-10 at lambda = 18. Using lambda = 18 for width-10 improves robust accuracy from 54.18% at lambda = 6 to 56.43%, an average gain of 2.25 percentage points, and raises perturbation stability from 67.25% to 72.11%. For standard adversarial training, the best robust accuracy is 49.24% for width-1 at lambda = 1.25, 54.36% for width-5 at lambda = 1.75, and 55.78% for width-10 at lambda = 2.00. On CIFAR100, the larger-regularization strategy raises the standard PGD robust accuracy of the widest model from 30.48% to 32.98%. Reported values are means over three runs, with standard deviations generally below 0.8 percentage points.
[context]
Experiments use CIFAR10 with 50,000 training and 10,000 test images, WideResNet-34 with widen factors 1, 5, and 10, batch size 128, 100 epochs, initial learning rate 0.1, and a learning-rate schedule that halves the rate every epoch after epoch 75. Robustness is evaluated with a 20-step PGD attack, step size 0.007, and epsilon 8/255; experiments are run on a single NVIDIA V100 GPU and repeated three times. Additional experiments use CIFAR100 and DenseNet-BC-40 with different growth rates, and attacks include PGD, C&W, FAB, and Square. The setting is image classification, not causal treatment-effect estimation, so it does not directly use CATE, PEHE, IPM, or treatment/control balancing, but it provides evidence that stronger smoothness- or Lipschitz-related regularization can improve worst-case perturbation performance and that regularization strength should be tuned as model capacity or width changes.
