The paper uses a weighted empirical risk minimization procedure, called WERM-ID-R, for identifiable causal effects. It takes a causal graph and finite observational samples, rewrites the target causal effect as a weighted distribution, estimates the ideal weight function by recursively estimating conditional and weighted distributions with gradient boosting, and then learns a regularized approximate weight to reduce variance before learning a prediction function by minimizing a regularized weighted empirical risk. The experimental setup compares WERM-ID-R, a simpler WERM-ID variant that directly minimizes the weighted empirical risk, and a plug-in gradient boosting estimator on synthetic structural causal model data for three causal graphs, including a job-training program graph and a heart-disease treatment graph. This method relates to the proposed RHTE objective only through the weighted empirical loss component for causal effect estimation; it does not include Lipschitz regularization, IPM representation balancing, or covariate-perturbation robustness.
