- in_text_citation: [(Shalit et al., 2016-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/17409ae85c237983dc22a5e4750d8054e4b6edc9)
- venue: International Conference on Machine Learning
- citation count: 1376
[method]
The related method in the paper is a counterfactual regression framework that jointly learns a covariate representation and two separate outcome heads for treated and control predictions. It minimizes an objective consisting of a weighted empirical factual loss, an Integral Probability Metric penalty between the treated and control representation distributions, and a model complexity term. The IPM is instantiated using Wasserstein distance or maximum mean discrepancy, computed on minibatches, and the representation is normalized by projection or batch normalization with fixed scale to control sensitivity to scaling. The model is implemented as a feed-forward neural network with three fully connected ELU layers; for the Jobs dataset all layers use 200 units, while for IHDP the representation uses 200 units and the hypothesis uses 100 units. Training uses Adam, with small l2 weight decay on hypothesis layers, mean squared error for continuous outcomes, and log-loss for binary outcomes. Experiments use the semi-synthetic IHDP dataset with 747 units, 25 covariates, 139 treated and 608 control units, simulated outcome setting A, 1000 outcome realizations, and 63/27/10 train/validation/test splits. Imbalance is increased by removing 347 observations from each split, leaving 400 units, using propensity-score-based removal probability q. The Jobs dataset uses 297 treated units, 425 experimental control units, and 2490 comparison control units, with 8 covariates and a binary unemployment outcome in which 482 subjects are unemployed; evaluation uses 10 splits with 56/24/20 ratios. Metrics include PEHE, absolute error in average treatment effect or average treatment effect on the treated, and policy risk as a function of treatment threshold. Baselines include ordinary least squares, logistic regression, k-nearest neighbors, targeted maximum likelihood estimation, Bayesian additive regression trees, random forests, causal forests, and balancing linear or neural methods. This paper method directly corresponds to the proposed weighted empirical loss plus IPM component, but it does not include the proposed Lipschitz regularization for robustness to covariate perturbation.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not report numeric PEHE, ATT error, or policy-risk values. It reports that on IHDP the IPM-regularized counterfactual regression variants, especially the Wasserstein version, benefit from treatment-control imbalance and retain a significant relative gain when imbalance is increased through propensity-based subsampling. It also reports that nonlinear estimators outperform linear ones for individual treatment effect estimation on IHDP. On Jobs, the gain from IPM penalties is smaller because evaluation is restricted to the randomized subset, where treated and control distributions are more similar. Logistic regression performs well for average treatment effect estimation but implies only a uniform treat-everyone policy, while causal forests and the counterfactual regression method achieve lower policy risk. k-nearest neighbors has strong within-sample performance on Jobs but poor out-of-sample generalization. These outcomes support the use of IPM-based representation balancing for imbalanced observational data, but they do not quantify the additional robustness benefit of adding Lipschitz regularization, which is the central extension in the proposed robust heterogeneous treatment effect plan.
[context]
Key experimental conditions include strong ignorability, binary treatment, bounded covariates, and factual observation of only one potential outcome per unit. The IPM coefficient is treated as a hyperparameter because the theoretical bound constant is not computable, and representation scaling is controlled by normalization. No hardware, runtime, or computational resource details are provided. The proposed research plan reuses the same general architecture scale, with a 200-unit representation function and a 100-unit prediction function, and also uses IPM-based representation balancing and weighted empirical loss. However, the proposed plan extends the objective with Lipschitz regularization, orthonormality or RKHS constraints, and adversarial spherical perturbation testing. The paper therefore provides a relevant baseline for the factual-loss plus IPM portion of the proposed method, while the robustness to covariate perturbation and Lipschitz-controlled worst-case loss are not evaluated in the paper.
