[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Chikahara, and Ushiyama, 2024-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/679a8718d4b5fa0eeb57052aafa620aaf2b9df8a)
- venue: Conference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 2
[method]
The paper evaluates a differentiable Pareto-smoothed weighting method for high-dimensional heterogeneous treatment effect estimation. The approach is a weighted representation-learning model with separate encoders for instrumental, confounder, and adjustment features, a propensity score model, and outcome models. It first fits a generalized Pareto distribution to the largest inverse propensity weights using probability-weighted moment estimators. Because ranking weights is non-differentiable, it replaces the rank operation with a continuous differentiable rank approximation and replaces indicator functions with sigmoid functions to make the GPD parameter estimators differentiable. Each raw weight is then replaced by a Pareto-smoothed GPD quantile clipped to the unit interval, and these smoothed weights are used in the weighted representation-learning objective. The objective is optimized end-to-end with an Adam-style stochastic gradient descent procedure by alternating minimization of the propensity, representation, and outcome objectives. This is related to the proposed RHTE method because both use a weighted empirical loss inside a representation-learning HTE objective, but the paper targets extreme propensity-weight instability rather than covariate perturbation, and the provided text does not include Lipschitz regularization or an explicit IPM term. It can be viewed as a complementary weight-robustness method for the RHTE plan, whereas the plan’s robustness is to covariate perturbations via Lipschitz control and IPM-based representation balance.
[results]
On semi-synthetic News and ACIC data, the proposed method and its self-normalized variant outperformed all 15 baselines in test PEHE. The excerpt reports PEHE values of 2.10 ± 0.66 and 0.52 ± 0.16, and states that the self-normalized variant had lower PEHE than the unnormalized variant. On synthetic data with known feature roles, the method and the weighted representation baseline learned feature-specific encoders, with larger absolute first-layer weights for the relevant feature blocks than for irrelevant blocks, while TARNet did not separate features. The method achieved the lowest test PEHE among the representation-learning methods shown, and a separate propensity-score learning baseline performed much worse on News, indicating that fitting propensity scores directly to high-dimensional features caused substantial misspecification. The synthetic results are reported qualitatively in the provided excerpt; no numeric PEHE values are given for that setting.
[context]
Experiments used high-dimensional semi-synthetic News data with n=5000 and d=3477 word-count features, 50 outcome realizations, and 60/20/20 train/validation/test splits; ACIC data with n=5000, d=177 features, 20 datasets, and the same split. Synthetic experiments used n=20000, 20 datasets, 50/25/25 splits, and feature dimensions partitioned into thirds for instrumental, confounder, and adjustment variables, with additional tests at d=600 to 3000. Models were three-layer feed-forward networks for representations, propensity score, and outcome models; hyperparameters including the differentiable-rank epsilon were tuned on validation data. Evaluation used PEHE, and the differentiable rank computation had O(B log B) cost per mini-batch. No hardware details were provided.
