The paper evaluates unitary recurrent neural networks whose recurrence matrix is constrained to be unitary, i.e., orthonormal in the complex sense, and compares a full-capacity uRNN optimized on the Stiefel manifold against a restricted-capacity uRNN and LSTM. The full-capacity model enforces orthonormality of the recurrence matrix through a Stiefel-manifold update with a fixed learning rate and no gradient normalization in the synthetic experiments, while other parameters are optimized with RMSprop. In the system identification task, only the recurrence matrix is optimized and all other parameters are fixed to oracle values. This is related to the proposed orthonormality regularization because both approaches constrain weight matrices to be orthonormal or unitary, which bounds the spectral norm and can help control smoothness; however, the paper uses a hard manifold constraint for recurrent dynamics rather than a soft Parseval-tightness Lipschitz regularizer for robust heterogeneous treatment effect estimation.
