[method]
The paper's relevant method is a Wasserstein GAN with gradient penalty, with weight clipping used as a baseline weight-based Lipschitz constraint. The gradient-penalty method enforces the critic's Lipschitz constraint by penalizing deviations of the critic's gradient norm from one on interpolated points between real and generated samples, rather than by clipping weights. This is related to the proposed orthonormality regularization because both are practical mechanisms for controlling model smoothness or Lipschitz behavior in neural networks, although the paper uses a gradient penalty and weight clipping rather than orthonormal weight matrices or Parseval tightness. Experiments compare the gradient-penalty method against weight clipping and DCGAN on CIFAR-10 and LSUN bedrooms, and also use a continuous-generator character-level language model on Google Billion Word. The language model uses a 1D CNN generator that maps a latent vector to 32 one-hot character vectors through 1D convolutions and a softmax output without sampling; the critic is a 1D CNN, and samples are decoded by taking the argmax of each output vector. Evaluation uses Inception score over training and at convergence for image generation, and qualitative sample inspection for language modeling.
[results]
On CIFAR-10, the gradient-penalty method trained with the same RMSProp optimizer and learning rate as weight clipping converged faster and reached a better Inception score than weight clipping; using Adam with a higher learning rate further improved performance. Compared with DCGAN, the gradient-penalty method converged more slowly in wall-clock time but produced a more stable Inception score at convergence. The best unsupervised CIFAR-10 result reported for the paper's ResNet model was 7.86 ± 0.07 Inception score, compared with 5.62 for DCGAN, 6.16 ± 0.07 for Improved GAN, 6.86 ± 0.06 for EGAN-Ent-VI, and 7.72 ± 0.13 for DFM. With label information, the same architecture was reported to outperform all other published models except SGAN, although no numeric SGAN value is provided in the excerpt. For LSUN bedrooms, the paper reports comparable sample quality to the standard GAN objective for equivalent architectures and improved quality when stability allowed wider architectures, but the excerpt does not provide numeric LSUN scores. For the character-level language model, samples showed frequent spelling errors but captured language statistics; the authors state that comparable results were not obtained with the standard GAN objective and a continuous generator.
