Experimental conditions included CIFAR-10 and LSUN bedrooms image datasets and Google Billion Word character sequences truncated to 32 characters. Image experiments used Wasserstein GAN variants with either weight clipping or gradient penalty, a ResNet architecture for the strongest CIFAR-10 result, and optimizer comparisons between RMSProp and Adam; exact learning rates, training time, and hardware are not specified in the excerpt. The language model used deterministic 1D CNN generator and critic components, softmax outputs without discrete sampling, and argmax decoding. The paper also references one-sided penalty experiments and LSUN robustness hyperparameters, but the provided excerpt does not include their detailed values. This context is relevant to the research plan because it demonstrates that Lipschitz regularization can improve training stability and sample quality in neural generative models, supporting the plan's broader use of Lipschitz control for robustness; however, the paper does not evaluate orthonormality regularization, causal heterogeneous treatment effect estimation, covariate perturbation, integral probability metric balancing, or treatment-effect metrics such as PEHE.
