[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical accuracy, generalization-gap, or perturbation-error values. It reports that spectral norm regularization produced the best generalizability among vanilla training, weight decay, adversarial training, and spectral norm regularization, and that it trained models insensitive to both training and test data perturbations. In the Hessian experiment, vanilla models showed larger maximum Hessian eigenvalues in the large-batch regime, but regularized models, especially those trained with spectral norm regularization, had larger maximum eigenvalues on both training and test data despite better generalizability. The authors conclude that the maximum Hessian eigenvalue is not a suggestive predictor of generalizability in this setting.
