[method]
The paper evaluates a Lipschitz-based certification method for ReLU networks, Fast-Lip, which upper-bounds the local Lipschitz constant of a network over an l_p perturbation ball around an input by propagating activation-status bounds through the layers and then uses that Lipschitz upper bound to produce a certified lower bound on the minimum adversarial distortion. It is compared with a global Op-norm spectral-norm Lipschitz bound, LP and LP-Full solvers, Reluplex, CLEVER, and attack-based upper bounds. This is related to the proposed orthonormality regularization because both concern controlling or measuring network smoothness through spectral/Lipschitz quantities; the paper provides a post-hoc evaluation of the robustness property that an orthonormality regularizer would be designed to improve in a causal representation learner.
[results]
On 2- and 3-layer MNIST toy networks with 20 neurons per layer, the paper reports that the certified lower bounds from Fast-Lin/Fast-Lip are within a 2-3x gap of exact Reluplex minimum distortions and are more than 10,000x faster. Compared with LP and LP-Full, the quality gap is within 35% and usually around 10%, with speedups of 33-14,000x. On large networks, Fast-Lip remains feasible while LP is infeasible; for example, on MNIST 2x1024 with l_inf perturbations, Fast-Lip gives a certified lower bound of 0.02512 versus LP 0.03386 and Op-norm 0.00263, with runtime 219 ms versus 20.8 s for LP, a 133x speedup. For l2 on the same network, Fast-Lip gives 0.59033 versus LP 0.75164 and Op-norm 0.40201, with 234 ms versus 195 s, a 1523x speedup. In deeper/larger CIFAR and MNIST networks, Op-norm often becomes zero or negative, while Fast-Lin/Fast-Lip still produce positive l_inf bounds, e.g., 0.00170 and 0.00030 for CIFAR 5x2048, though these are much smaller than the attack upper bound of 0.02351. The paper also reports that adversarially trained networks yield significantly larger certified lower bounds, but the excerpt does not provide the corresponding Table 2 values.
[context]
The experimental setting is classification robustness on MNIST and CIFAR fully connected ReLU networks, not causal HTE estimation. Toy networks have 2-3 layers and 20 neurons per layer; large networks have 2-7 layers and 1024 or 2048 neurons per layer. Bounds are averaged over 100 random test images with random attack targets for l_1, l_2, and l_inf perturbations. Runtime is measured per image on a single CPU core. Baselines include LP, LP-Full, Reluplex, CLEVER, Op-norm, and CW/EAD attacks; LP-Full and Reluplex are computationally infeasible for the large networks. The paper does not test orthonormality regularization, parseval tightness constraints, or covariate perturbation in treatment-effect models, so its results are relevant as evidence that Lipschitz/spectral-norm bounds can certify robustness under input perturbation, but they do not directly validate the proposed regularizer for robust HTE or PEHE performance.
