On CIFAR-10, SN-SWGAN and SN-SMMDGAN performed comparably to SN-GAN. On CelebA, SN-SWGAN and SN-SMMDGAN dramatically outperformed other same-architecture methods in all three metrics, trained faster, and were consistent over multiple initializations. SN-SWGAN also far outperformed WGAN-GP on both CIFAR-10 and CelebA. ImageNet scores for SMMDGAN and SN-SMMDGAN were reported against pretrained BGAN and SN-GAN, but the supplied excerpt does not include the numeric table values, so the exact IS, FID, and KID magnitudes cannot be reported. Figure 2 reports KID over generator iterations, singular values for layer 2, critic complexity, and condition number for layer 1, but the excerpt does not provide numeric point values. Spectral-normalized WGAN and Sobolev-GAN were unstable and did not converge despite many attempts. Spectral-normalized MMDGAN converged on CIFAR-10 but was unstable on CelebA, suggesting that spectral normalization alone may be too loose for those losses. Adding learned gamma with a gradient penalty and an L2 penalty on critic activations generally behaved like MMDGAN and did not substantially improve performance; CelebA runs were aborted early when training failed. Figure 2(c) indicates that the expected critic gradient is much better controlled by SMMD even when spectral normalization is used.
