[method]
The paper does not use orthonormality or Parseval tightness regularization; the closest related method is spectral normalisation, a weight-constraint regularizer that bounds the spectral radius of selected layers to control layer Lipschitz constants. Spectral normalisation approximates each selected layer's spectral radius with one power iteration per forward pass and applies a hard projection W_hat = W / max(lambda, rho), with lambda set to 1 unless otherwise stated; convolutional layers use convolutional and transposed convolutional operations instead of matrix-vector products. The experiments compare DQN baselines with spectral normalisation applied to the last hidden layer, last two hidden layers, or last three hidden layers, and also study two proxies: DIVOUT, which scales the unnormalised network output by the inverse spectral radius, and DIVGRAD, which scales gradients by the inverse spectral radius before the optimiser. Smoothness is measured by taking the best checkpoint for each game-architecture combination, sampling 100k steps, and computing the maximum norm of the gradient of the state-action value with respect to the state. Alternative regularisers, Gradient Penalty and Batch Normalisation, are also tested as comparisons for smoothness control.
[results]
In a large MinAtar sweep of 14,000 DQN agents across six depths and widths, many learning rates and epsilon values, Adam and RMSProp, four games, and two seeds per configuration, spectral normalisation almost never degrades the unnormalised baseline, extends the usable range of Adam hyperparameters, and on a 15M-step run continues improving while the baseline plateaus. In a smoothness study with 2,400 trained agents across 12 model sizes, DQN and spectral-normalisation variants, four games, and 10 seeds per combination, spectral normalisation does not consistently produce smoother networks; only a slight Spearman rank correlation is reported, with no coefficient given, and the performance-smoothness relationship varies by game, being strong in Space Invaders and absent in Breakout. A three-layer baseline has a higher empirical Jacobian norm than normalised models, but for deeper models the best normalised agents have smoothness similar to the baseline while achieving higher performance. Gradient Penalty generally hurts performance or makes no difference, and Batch Normalisation degrades performance. DIVOUT recovers the performance of spectral normalisation and fails when spectral normalisation fails, while DIVGRAD is reported as sufficient to explain the performance gain and can improve DQN even when spectral normalisation fails, such as when all hidden layers are normalised. Exact score values and statistical significance tests are not provided in the excerpt.
[context]
Most experiments use MinAtar because large-scale ALE studies would be prohibitive; MinAtar reproduces five Atari-like games with non-stationary dynamics but less visual complexity, and reported MinAtar results are usually averages of normalised scores over four games. Agents use at least one convolutional layer and two final linear layers; depth changes the number of convolutional layers and width changes feature maps and linear units. Atari evaluation uses 54 of 57 games because one ROM is unavailable, one crashes, and one is excluded for score dominance and noise. Spectral normalisation uses one power iteration, hard projection, lambda=1, and selected layers labelled [-2], [-2,-3], or [-2,-3,-4]. The optimisation-proxy analysis assumes a feed-forward estimator with ReLU layers, a subset of normalised layers, and a Huber TD-error loss in DQN; bias scaling is used to derive the DIVOUT equivalence. This relates to the proposed orthonormality regularisation and robust HTE plan because both aim to bound spectral norms or Lipschitz constants to control model smoothness under perturbation, but the paper shows that spectral-norm control in DQN can improve learning mainly through optimisation effects rather than guaranteed smoothness, so an orthonormality regularizer for HTE should be evaluated for both Lipschitz bounds and downstream robustness to covariate perturbation.
