[method]
The paper develops and evaluates regularization-based kernel machines in which the objective combines an empirical loss with a gamma-weighted squared RKHS norm. In the multi-task linear setting, the method represents n task functions with a shared feature vector and task-specific linear maps, producing a multi-task kernel over inputs augmented with a task index. The experiments compare single-task SVM/regression, one SVM for all tasks, a simple multi-task SVM using an identity task-coupling matrix, and a multi-task SVM using a PCA-estimated task-coupling matrix. The customer data experiment uses linear SVM classification with hinge loss, while the school data experiment uses linear SVM regression with epsilon-loss. This is related to the proposed RKHS regularization in the robust HTE plan because both use an RKHS norm as a smoothness/complexity constraint, although the paper does not explicitly target Lipschitz constants, adversarial covariate perturbation, or heterogeneous treatment effect estimation.
[results]
On the customer choice data, when there are few data per task, both multi-task SVM variants significantly outperform the single-task SVM. For 20 training points per task, the multi-task SVM reduces error relative to the single-task SVM by 1.14, 1.56, and 2.07 percentage points for 50, 100, and 200 tasks, respectively. When there are 90 training points per task, the simple identity-matrix multi-task SVM does not improve over the single-task SVM, but the PCA-based multi-task SVM significantly outperforms the other methods. Training one SVM on all tasks gives poor performance, with test error between 38 and 42 percent. With few tasks, multi-task learning can hurt performance if the task-coupling parameter lambda is too small; for 200 tasks and 20 data per task, the error has a flat minimum between lambda = 0.4 and 0.6, and any lambda between 0.2 and 1 beats the single-task SVM, while for 10 tasks only lambda between 0.7 and 1 does. On the school exam data, simultaneous multi-task learning outperforms learning each school separately, and the simple identity-matrix multi-task method significantly outperforms the compared Bayesian multi-task method. One SVM for all tasks performs best on this data set, matching performance obtained with lambda between 0 and 0.7, indicating that the method is not very sensitive to lambda in that range.
[context]
The customer data experiment uses 200 individuals, each with 120 choice observations. The original three product attributes are transformed into 20 binary features. Experiments vary the number of tasks to 50, 100, or 200 and the number of training points per task to 20, 30, 60, or 90, with the remaining points used for testing. The single-task SVM regularization parameter gamma is selected from 0.1, 1, or 10 using test error. For multi-task learning, gamma and lambda are selected using one validation point per task, then that point is added back to training. Lambda is selected from 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, or 0.8. The PCA-based method initializes the task-coupling matrix as identity, iteratively estimates task parameters, runs PCA on the estimated parameters, keeps enough principal components to retain at least 90 percent of the total energy, and typically converges in 4 to 5 iterations. The school data experiment uses 15,362 students from 139 schools, with 27 input features after dummy coding. Each school is treated as one task. The data are split 75 percent training and 25 percent testing in 10 random splits. The method uses a linear kernel, gamma = 1, and the identity task-coupling matrix, with lambda varied. Performance is measured by explained variance on the test set. No hardware or computational resource details are reported.
