[method]
The paper uses a two-stage RKHS embedding method for MDPs. It first estimates conditional distribution embeddings of transition kernels from sampled transitions in a Gaussian RKHS, then uses the resulting linear expectation operators in dynamic programming for value estimation and policy optimization. The embedding estimator is a regularized kernel or vector-valued regression problem with a regularization parameter selected by cross-validation, which constrains the learned operator to a smooth kernel function class. This is methodologically related to the proposed RKHS regularization in the robust HTE plan because both use an RKHS function class and norm-based regularization to impose function-space smoothness, although the paper applies this to MDP expectation operators rather than to Lipschitz-robust CATE prediction under covariate perturbation.
[results]
In the 50 by 50 navigation task, value-function error decreased as the number of training transitions increased from 1000 to 5000, demonstrating convergence. In the pendulum swing-up task, the embedding-based value estimator had lower prediction error than NPDP, especially at larger sample sizes, and was 50 to 110 times faster than NPDP. In policy learning, the embedding method outperformed GP-based LSPI, while LSPI improved slowly after about 500 samples. In the high-dimensional two-room image task, the embedding method improved with sample size and produced value estimates close to the optimal value at 4000 training points, while GP-based LSPI did not improve and had obvious difficulties; NPDP was not run because it was computationally intractable in that high-dimensional setting.
[context]
The experiments used synthetic MDPs rather than causal or treatment-effect datasets. The navigation task had a fully observed discrete state space, four actions, 80 percent action success, and 20 percent random movement. The pendulum task used angle in [-pi, pi], angular velocity in [-7, 7], torque in [-5, 5], and reward exp(-theta^2 - 0.2 omega^2). The high-dimensional task used four rendered camera orientations from a two-room environment and had no explicit state description. Training transitions were sampled uniformly, a Gaussian kernel was used, and in policy learning the kernel width was set to the average K-neighbor distance with K equal to one quarter of the sample size. The pendulum action space was discretized into 25 actions, policy value was evaluated on a 25 by 25 grid against optimal dynamic programming values, and results were averaged over 10 repetitions. The regularization parameter was cross-validated, and value-estimation hyperparameters including bandwidth and regularization were selected on a validation set with error reported on an independent test set. The paper does not test covariate perturbation, adversarial robustness, or treatment-effect estimation, so its relevance to the robust HTE plan is limited to the use of RKHS function-space regularization and kernel-based expectation estimation.
