[method]
The paper develops an RKHS-based balancing-weight estimator for functional treatment effects. It assumes the conditional mean outcome function m(a, x) lies in a tensor-product RKHS formed by a treatment RKHS and a covariate RKHS, and uses the RKHS norm to define a balancing criterion that controls the discrepancy between the weighted empirical covariate distribution and the target distribution over functions with norm at most one. The criterion is implemented with an empirical norm evaluated at a set of evaluation points and is combined with a weight-magnitude regularization term controlled by a tuning parameter eta and an optional upper bound L. The final estimator forms a weighted kernel ridge regression type estimate of the functional treatment effect tau(a) = E_X m(a, X), after recovering unobserved functional treatments by estimated trajectories or kernel mean embeddings. In the real-data application, the functional treatment is the Gaussian-kernel mean embedding of each subject's activity-intensity distribution, and the covariates are age, education level, and family poverty income ratio. The smoothing parameter lambda is selected by leave-one-out cross-validation using adjusted responses from a fast FCBPS weighting procedure, and eta is selected using a fitted kernel ridge regression outcome model. The method is compared with NW, FCBPS, NPFCBPS, and REG.
[results]
In the simulation study with 200 datasets and three settings, out-of-sample MSE was measured on new evaluation points. NW had the worst performance in all settings. FCBPS and NPFCBPS were worse than CFB and REG in Settings 2 and 3, where the linear outcome-model assumption is violated; in Setting 1, which satisfies the linear assumption, FCBPS and NPFCBPS still underperformed CFB because they only balance linear outcome models. CFB achieved the smallest average MSE among the five estimators except in Setting 2, where REG was better. CFB also had the smallest standard errors in all three settings, indicating greater stability. The provided excerpt does not include the numeric MSE values from Tables 1 and 2. In the NHANES application, all five estimators produced the same ordering of estimated BMI for three medoid activity-density curves, with more active profiles associated with lower BMI. NPFCBPS was similar to NW, while CFB, REG, and FCBPS were more similar to each other and showed a larger BMI difference between curve IDs 33387 and 32104. For obesity categorization, NW and NPFCBPS classified almost all subjects as overweight or obese, and NW produced a counter-intuitive split within the obese group; CFB, REG, and FCBPS showed a steady inverse relationship between strenuous activity and BMI. The excerpt does not provide the numeric BMI values from Table 3.
