The simulation context includes 200 simulated datasets, three settings, and out-of-sample evaluation on new points; Setting 1 is linear while Settings 2 and 3 violate the linear model assumption. The real-data context is NHANES 2005-2006 physical activity monitoring for white males aged 20-50, with 427 subjects after preprocessing. Preprocessing removed unreliable observations, intensity values above 1000 or equal to 0, and subjects with fewer than 100 remaining intensity observations or missing covariates. The outcome is BMI, covariates are age, education level, and family poverty income ratio, and treatments are Gaussian-kernel mean embeddings of activity-intensity distributions. Three representative curves were selected by k-medoid clustering with k=3, and BMI categories were underweight/healthy, overweight, and obese. The paper does not report hardware, software, or computational runtime details. The method is related to the proposed RKHS regularization in the robust HTE plan because it uses an RKHS norm as a function-space smoothness constraint in causal effect estimation, but it does not test adversarial covariate perturbation, orthonormality regularization, or Lipschitz bounds for neural-network HTE models.
