[method]
The paper evaluates a balancing neural network (BNN) for binary-treatment counterfactual inference and individual treatment effect estimation. The model learns a representation of the covariates and an outcome function jointly, using fully connected ReLU layers. Two variants are tested: BNN-4-0, which uses four representation-only ReLU layers followed by a single linear output layer, and BNN-2-2, which uses two representation-only ReLU layers, then two ReLU output layers after the treatment indicator is added, followed by a linear output layer. The BNN-2-2 architecture allows treatment-by-covariate interactions and is intended to capture heterogeneous individual effects. Training uses RM-SProp with a small l2 weight decay and an imbalance penalty that encourages the factual and counterfactual representation distributions to be similar. The experiments use semi-simulated IHDP and News data with known counterfactual outcomes, and hyperparameters are selected using held-out repeated simulations rather than standard cross-validation on the final test set. Evaluation uses RMSE of the estimated individual treatment effect, absolute error in average treatment effect, and PEHE. This paper method is related to the proposed perturbation-testing method because BNN is one of the heterogeneous treatment effect baselines named in the research plan, and the paper’s semi-simulated PEHE-based evaluation provides a comparable clean-covariate benchmark. However, the paper does not test uniform noise, FGSM, or OTCM covariate perturbations, so it does not directly evaluate robustness to adversarial or measurement-error covariate shifts.
