[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Chu et al., 2020-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fed8480b9a1ec0689e1ae237f008eb05b4a78eca)
- venue: International Conference on Information and Knowledge Management
- citation count: 39
[method]
The paper evaluates FSRM, a feature-selection representation matching method for treatment effect estimation. It uses a deep network with a sparse one-to-one input feature-selection layer and elastic-net regularization in fully connected representation layers, predicts observed outcomes and treatment assignment, and matches treated and control units in the learned representation space using Mahalanobis, Euclidean, or propensity-score distance. A Wasserstein or integral probability metric term balances the treatment and control representation distributions. Experiments use IHDP with 747 units and 25 covariates, modified IHDP with 35 additional irrelevant variables, and a synthetic dataset with 60 variables comprising 15 confounders, 15 adjustment variables, 10 instrumental variables, and 20 irrelevant variables. The main evaluation is repeated over 1000 realizations and compares FSRM against k-nearest neighbors, causal forests, random forests, BART, GANITE, propensity score matching, TARNET, CFRNET with Wasserstein distance, SITE, and perfect match. The evaluation metrics are ATE error and square-root PEHE. A separate robustness experiment increases treatment selection bias by conditional sampling with probability q from 0 to 1 and compares FSRM Euclidean against CFRNET WASS, SITE, and PM. Ablations remove either the feature-selection layers or the IPM term, and sensitivity analysis varies the weights for outcome prediction, smoothness, and sparsity. This relates to the proposed RHTE plan because it uses representation learning and IPM-based distribution balancing for heterogeneous treatment effect estimation and tests robustness, but it does not implement the proposed covariate perturbation tests with uniform noise, FGSM, or OTCM, nor does it use Lipschitz regularization.
[results]
FSRM Euclidean achieved the lowest ATE error on all three datasets: 0.05 ± 0.01 on IHDP, 0.06 ± 0.01 on modified IHDP, and 0.01 ± 0.01 on the synthetic dataset. It also achieved the lowest square-root PEHE on modified IHDP with 1.31 ± 0.10 and on the synthetic dataset with 0.13 ± 0.01. On IHDP, FSRM Euclidean had square-root PEHE of 1.24 ± 0.04, which was better than k-nearest neighbors at 4.10 ± 0.20, causal forests at 3.80 ± 0.20, random forests at 6.60 ± 0.30, BART at 2.30 ± 0.10, GANITE at 2.40 ± 0.40, and propensity score matching at 2.70 ± 3.85, but worse than TARNET at 0.95 ± 0.02, CFRNET WASS at 0.76 ± 0.02, SITE at 0.66 ± 0.11, and perfect match at 0.84 ± 0.61. FSRM Euclidean outperformed FSRM Mahalanobis, which had IHDP square-root PEHE 1.32 ± 0.05 and ATE error 0.07 ± 0.01, modified IHDP square-root PEHE 1.39 ± 0.08 and ATE error 0.07 ± 0.02, and synthetic square-root PEHE 0.15 ± 0.01 and ATE error 0.01 ± 0.01. FSRM Euclidean also outperformed FSRM propensity, which had IHDP square-root PEHE 3.05 ± 0.15 and ATE error 0.09 ± 0.01, modified IHDP square-root PEHE 3.09 ± 0.42 and ATE error 0.09 ± 0.02, and synthetic square-root PEHE 0.20 ± 0.01 and ATE error 0.01 ± 0.01. In the selection-bias experiment, FSRM Euclidean consistently outperformed CFRNET WASS, SITE, and PM as q increased from 0 to 1, although no numeric values are reported. Removing the feature-selection layers or the IPM term degraded performance, and adding outcome prediction and L1/L2 penalties improved stability and accuracy, but the paper does not report quantitative values for these ablations or sensitivity analyses.
[context]
The experimental setting is semi-synthetic and synthetic treatment-effect estimation, not adversarial covariate measurement error. IHDP contains 747 units with 608 controls and 139 treated units. Modified IHDP adds 35 irrelevant variables sampled from a multivariate normal distribution. The synthetic dataset uses a partially linear outcome model and 60 covariates with different causal roles. All main results are averaged over 1000 realizations. Baseline hyperparameters follow the original papers, while FSRM hyperparameters are selected on validation data using a standardized search; the exact search ranges are not fully specified in the provided text. The robustness experiment changes treatment selection bias by sampling units with extreme propensity-score deviation from 0.5 with probability q, not by perturbing covariates. Hardware and software details are not reported. Relative to the proposed method, this paper provides evidence that IPM-based representation balancing and feature selection can improve treatment-effect estimation under selection bias and irrelevant variables, but it does not directly test the proposed uniform covariate noise, FGSM, or OTCM adversarial perturbations, so its robustness findings cannot be taken as direct validation of the RHTE covariate-perturbation claim.
[end related work 4]

