[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Guo et al., 2022-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8ac58d90cfde42ffeb5b8c6032dcdc39f495f14d)
- venue: CLEaR
- citation count: 23
[method]
The paper uses a robust-optimization partial-identification method, abbreviated RCI, for average treatment effect estimation under noisy covariates. It begins with a parametric model for the joint distribution or relevant conditionals, fits parameters by maximum likelihood, and then replaces the point ATE estimate with an interval obtained by optimizing the causal identification functional over an uncertainty set of distributions consistent with the observed noisy data and a total-variation distance bound. The uncertainty set is expressed through the unobserved conditional distribution of the noiseless covariates given the observed variables. The paper instantiates this robust formulation for backdoor adjustment, inverse propensity weighting, and frontdoor adjustment, and mentions an extension to double machine learning in the appendix. For backdoor adjustment, the robust objective is the difference between expected fitted outcome-model values under treatment and control, where expectations are taken over uncertain conditional covariate distributions. The robust optimization is solved using a projected gradient descent ascent algorithm for the distributionally robust optimization formulation. This method is related to the proposed research plan because both address causal estimation under covariate perturbation and both use IHDP-style and ACIC-style benchmarks, but the paper targets ATE partial-identification intervals under Gaussian measurement noise rather than CATE estimation under uniform, FGSM, or OTCM adversarial perturbations, and it does not use representation learning or Lipschitz regularization.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numeric table values. Across synthetic and real-data experiments, RCI produced partial-identification intervals with improved coverage of the true ATE as noise increased, while the naive estimator applied directly to noisy covariates had low coverage and was highly sensitive to noise. RCI was also described as not being overly conservative. On the IHDP case study, the frontdoor-adjustment version of RCI achieved higher coverage than in the synthetic frontdoor experiments, which the paper attributes to the outcome being linearly correlated with the mediator and the use of a matching linear parametric model. No PEHE values, FGSM or OTCM perturbation results, CATE estimates, or adversarial robustness metrics are reported, so the results are not directly comparable to the proposed RHTE evaluation.
[context]
Synthetic experiments include binary logistic outcome models, a nonlinear continuous-outcome synthetic dataset, and frontdoor datasets with single and multiple mediators. Noisy covariates are generated by adding Gaussian noise to each dimension of the true covariates, and the noise strength is represented by a total-variation distance parameter used in the RCI uncertainty set. The real ACIC case study uses a 2019 spambase-based ACIC dataset with 2000 examples and 22 continuous covariates, which is different from the 4802-unit and 82-covariate ACIC dataset described in the proposed research plan. Noisy covariates in the ACIC case study use five Gaussian noise levels with means 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, and 0.5 and standard deviations 0.5, 0.5, 1, 1, and 1. The IHDP case study uses 747 samples and 25 covariates from the NPCI package, with simulated mediator and outcome generation and the same five noise levels. Evaluation uses coverage probabilities averaged over 100 trials with standard error. The excerpt does not report hardware specifications, exact optimizer hyperparameters, runtime details, or PEHE-based evaluation metrics.
[end related work 5]

