The paper uses a sensitivity-analysis-driven adversarial-like robustness method for learning-based steering. It perturbs base driving images with Gaussian blur, additive white Gaussian noise, radial distortion, and RGB/HSV channel brightness shifts. Instead of comparing raw perturbation parameters directly, it computes the Fréchet Inception Distance between perturbed and base datasets to create a unified perturbation space, and defines sensitivity as the derivative of mean absolute steering error with respect to FID. It then discretizes perturbation levels, selects for each factor the perturbed dataset that gives the lowest validation mean absolute error, and iteratively retrains a steering model on the base data plus these selected worst-case perturbed datasets. This relates to the proposed research method and overall plan because both use controlled perturbations and worst-case or adversarial-style training to improve robustness. However, the paper addresses image-quality perturbations for a steering regression task, not covariate perturbations for heterogeneous treatment effect estimation, and it does not use Lipschitz regularization, spherical adversarial perturbations, or causal evaluation metrics.
[results]
The paper reports that all tested image-quality factors degrade steering performance as perturbation level increases. The largest reported degradation exceeds 50% mean absolute error loss for the lighter level-5 green-channel perturbation. In the FID-aligned comparison, channel-level perturbations are more harmful than image-level blur, noise, and distortion. Blur and noise are identified as the least sensitive factors, while the V channel is the most sensitive. Darker and lighter perturbations of the same channel produce different mean absolute error values at similar FID values. The excerpt does not provide baseline comparisons, statistical significance tests, confidence intervals, or causal effect estimation metrics such as PEHE.
