Processing method: Context: The plan targets robust CATE estimation when covariates are perturbed. Method: We will develop a representation learning-based Robust Heterogeneous Treatment Effect (RHTE) framework that integrates adversarial machine learning techniques into causal inference. Synthesis: This is the central system design that connects adversarial robustness, Lipschitz control, and treatment effect estimation.
Processing method: Context: The framework needs a formal worst-case covariate perturbation objective. Method: We will formulate the robustness problem using adversarial samples under spherical perturbation assumptions. For a given covariate x, we define the adversarial sample as the worst-case perturbation within an ε-ball that maximizes the loss function. We will study the Expected Adversarial Factual Loss, which controls estimation error in the worst-case scenario. Synthesis: This defines the adversarial loss that the rest of the plan uses to make HTE estimation robust to measurement error.
Processing method: Context: One proposed Lipschitz regularizer controls neural network weights. Method: Orthonormality Regularization: Based on parseval tightness theory, constraining weight matrices to be orthonormal to control spectral norms. Synthesis: This regularizer provides a practical way to bound model smoothness in the neural representation learner.
Processing method: Context: The training objective must combine causal estimation, robustness, and representation balance. Method: We will minimize an objective function that combines three components: weighted empirical loss (to balance treatment and control groups), Lipschitz regularization (to control model smoothness), and Integral Probability Metric (IPM) distance (to balance representation distributions between treatment groups). Synthesis: This optimization framework operationalizes the RHTE model by jointly optimizing factual accuracy, adversarial smoothness, and treatment-control representation balance.
Processing method: Context: The adversarial objective must be linked to standard causal estimation error. Method: We will establish the relationship between adversarial loss and standard factual loss through Lipschitz constants. Our theoretical analysis will show that the adversarial loss can be bounded by simultaneously controlling the expected factual loss and the Lipschitz constant of the model. Synthesis: This theoretical result motivates the proposed Lipschitz regularization and explains why smoothness control can improve robust CATE estimation.
Processing method: Context: The method must be evaluated across tabular, semi-synthetic, synthetic, and multimodal settings. Method: We will evaluate our method on four datasets: ACIC: A benchmark dataset with 4,802 units and 82 covariates from linked birth and infant death data; IHDP: A semi-synthetic dataset with 747 units and 25 covariates from the Infant Health and Development Program; UTK-sim: A synthetic multimodal dataset combining tabular data with UTK images (1,000 units, 2,710 covariates); TC-sim: A synthetic dataset based on toxic comment data (3,240 units, 3,071 covariates). Synthesis: These datasets provide the empirical testbeds for comparing robust HTE estimation across data types and covariate dimensions.
Processing method: Context: A second proposed Lipschitz regularizer uses function-space smoothness. Method: RKHS Regularization: Assuming the model lies in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space and controlling the RKHS norm. Synthesis: This regularizer offers an alternative smoothness constraint that can be compared with orthonormality regularization in the robust HTE model.
Processing method: Context: The robustness claim must be tested under realistic and adversarial covariate errors. Method: We will add controlled perturbations to test sets using uniform noise δx ~ U(-0.1, 0.1)^dim(x) to simulate covariate measurement errors. We will also test non-spherical perturbations using Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM) and One-step Target Class Method (OTCM). Synthesis: These perturbation tests directly evaluate whether the RHTE framework remains accurate when covariates are noisy or adversarially shifted.
