[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Johansson et al., 2016-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/759b00cf35b397eab468935b5d90d04e9ed25549)
- venue: International Conference on Machine Learning
- citation count: 892
[method]
The paper evaluates a balancing neural network (BNN) for counterfactual and heterogeneous treatment effect estimation, with two variants: BNN-4-0, which uses 4 ReLU representation-only layers followed by a single linear output layer, and BNN-2-2, which uses 2 ReLU representation-only layers, then concatenates the treatment indicator, then uses 2 ReLU output layers and a single linear output layer. The BNN is trained jointly on factual outcomes with an imbalance or balancing penalty applied to the learned representation, aiming to reduce the discrepancy between the factual and counterfactual distributions. BNN-4-0 is constrained by a linear outcome layer and therefore cannot model interactions between the representation and treatment, limiting its ability to capture heterogeneous individual treatment effects; BNN-2-2 adds non-linear output layers after treatment to allow such interactions. The paper also evaluates an unbalanced 4-layer feed-forward neural network, NN-4, which predicts the factual outcome from covariates and treatment without any balancing penalty. This NN-4 baseline is used to isolate the effect of the balancing regularization from generic neural network capacity. Experiments are conducted on the semi-simulated IHDP dataset and a simulated News dataset constructed from New York Times word counts and LDA topic representations. Evaluation metrics include RMSE of the estimated individual treatment effect, absolute error in the estimated average treatment effect, and PEHE.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not include the numerical values from the reported tables, so exact quantitative improvements cannot be stated. The reported comparative outcome is that non-linear methods generally perform better than linear methods for individual treatment effect estimation, measured by ITE RMSE and PEHE. BNN-2-2 is reported to perform best on both IHDP and News for ITE and PEHE, while remaining competitive on average treatment effect. BNN-4-0 is reported to outperform the unbalanced NN-4 on the News dataset, indicating that the balancing regularization improves counterfactual estimation relative to a standard neural network with similar non-linear capacity. A sensitivity analysis over the imbalance penalty alpha shows a performance valley near alpha = 1 and no loss in performance for smaller alpha values, supporting the intended effect of penalizing imbalance in the representation. The linear balancing method BLR and LASSO plus ridge regression are also reported to be strong for ITE estimation, but the key neural-network result is that the balancing penalty helps avoid overfitting the representation to the factual outcome.
[context]
IHDP is a semi-simulated benchmark dataset, while News is a newly introduced simulation with n = 5000 news items, 50 LDA topics trained on New York Times documents, a vocabulary of 3477 words, scaling factor C = 50, assignment bias kappa = 10, and 50 repeated outcome realizations. The BNN models use fully connected ReLU layers trained with RM-SProp and a small l2 weight decay of 1e-3. For IHDP, layers have 25 hidden units; for News, representation layers have 400 units and output layers have 200 units. The nearest-neighbor term mentioned in the broader balancing formulation was omitted because it did not improve empirical performance. Hyperparameters were selected once across repeated simulated experiments using a held-out set of experiments, rather than by standard cross-validation on the test set, because counterfactual outcomes are unavailable in real-world settings. This paper method relates to the proposed research plan because BNN is one of the named neural-network baselines, and its representation-balancing objective is conceptually analogous to the IPM-based treatment/control representation balancing component in the proposed RHTE framework. The NN-4 comparison is also relevant because it serves as a generic-capacity baseline, similar to the proposed goal of determining whether robustness gains come from specific adversarial or Lipschitz components rather than from generic model capacity. However, the paper’s BNN uses imbalance regularization rather than adversarial or Lipschitz regularization, so it is a baseline for balanced representation learning rather than a direct test of robustness to covariate perturbation.
[end related work 1]

