[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Knaus et al., 2018-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/11df421778d470bb3469ac010adbeaf6cd3298e0)
- venue: Econometrics Journal
- citation count: 178
[method]
The paper uses a simulation-based benchmark of generic heterogeneous treatment effect estimators that are directly relevant to the proposed baseline comparison. The estimators include conditional mean regressions, which estimate treated and control outcome means separately and then take their difference, analogous to T-learner or S-learner style baselines in the proposed method. It also includes modified outcome methods based on inverse probability weighting and doubly robust pseudo-outcomes, as well as MCM and R-learning implemented through weighted Lasso. All estimators are evaluated with two machine learning methods: Random Forests, representing local conditional-mean approximation, and Lasso, representing global conditional-mean approximation. The inputs are simulated covariates, binary treatment assignments, and outcomes under several data-generating processes labeled ITE0, ITE1, and ITE2, with selection or random assignment and with or without random noise. The implementation uses cross-fitting so that nuisance parameters and treatment-effect predictions are estimated on different samples. The evaluation setup predicts individual treatment effects for validation observations and reports mean squared error, absolute bias, and standard deviation. Aggregate GATE and ATE estimates are obtained by averaging predicted individual treatment effects. Hardware and computational resources are not reported.
[results]
Random Forest conditional mean regressions clearly outperform Lasso conditional mean regressions in mean squared error. The difference is especially large in the smaller-sample ITE0 case, where the Lasso version has more than three times the mean squared error of the Random Forest version. This advantage is mainly driven by lower mean standard deviation for the Random Forest version. For modified outcome methods, Random Forest versions are superior in smaller samples, and the doubly robust Random Forest version performs well because of relatively low mean standard deviation. The doubly robust Lasso version is the worst estimator in smaller samples, with up to twice the mean standard deviation of the next worst estimator and consequently very high mean squared error. In larger samples, the doubly robust Lasso version outperforms the other modified outcome methods for ITE0 and ITE1 and is close to the Random Forest version for ITE2. Inverse probability weighted modified outcomes have relatively high mean standard deviation and are not competitive, even though the simulated propensity scores are not extreme. Overall, doubly robust methods are generally preferred, except when Lasso is used in small samples.
[context]
The experimental conditions are Monte Carlo simulations rather than real healthcare, image, or toxic-comment data. The paper restricts the machine learning comparison to Random Forests and Lasso and uses only pure combinations, meaning all estimation steps use the same learner. Cross-fitting is used to reduce overfitting. In small-sample settings, the doubly robust outcome nuisance function is estimated from only 250 observations, while the other nuisance functions are estimated from 500 observations, which the authors identify as a likely source of Lasso instability. A Random Forest implementation for weighted minimization is not available, so MCM and R-learning are implemented only with Lasso. The paper also includes an infeasible benchmark that uses true individual treatment effects as the prediction target. These conditions are relevant to the proposed research plan because the plan also compares HTE baselines such as Random Forests and meta-learning approaches, but the paper does not test covariate perturbation, adversarial loss, Lipschitz regularization, integral probability metric balancing, neural representation learning, or the proposed datasets and sqrt(PEHE) metric.
