[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Kiriakidou, and Diou, 2022-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ec757f6200b9c423353d9bd8a01c9bbdb4982d06)
- venue: Hellenic Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 8
[method]
The paper uses a benchmarking and statistical evaluation framework for causal effect estimators rather than proposing a new HTE estimator. It evaluates six models: DragonNet, TARNet, NEDnet, R-Forest implemented as an S-learner with random forests, C-Forest, and BART. For each simulation, it records absolute ATE error and PEHE, computes a performance ratio by dividing each model's error by the best model's error on that simulation, and builds performance profiles showing the fraction of simulations within a factor of the best model. It then applies a nonparametric rank-based omnibus test to detect overall differences among models, followed by a multiple-comparison procedure with adjusted p-values at alpha = 0.05 to identify pairwise statistical differences. This relates to the proposed research method because it compares a subset of the proposed baselines, including DragonNet, TARNet, random-forest/S-learner, causal forest, and BART, on IHDP and a synthetic confounded dataset, but it does not implement the proposed RHTE adversarial or Lipschitz model and does not test robustness under covariate perturbation.
[results]
On IHDP, DragonNet was the top model for both ATE and PEHE. For ATE, the omnibus test statistic was 314.33 with p = 1.73e-10; DragonNet statistically outperformed all tree-based models and NEDnet, was not statistically different from TARNet, and TARNet outperformed R-Forest, BART, and NEDnet while being similar to C-Forest. For PEHE, the omnibus test statistic was 1029.74 with p = 0; average ranks were DragonNet 2.098, TARNet 2.782, R-Forest 3.150, NEDnet 3.166, BART 4.372, and C-Forest 5.432. Performance profiles showed DragonNet and TARNet achieved the lowest PEHE in 26% and 25% of simulations, NEDnet and R-Forest in 22% and 17%, and BART and C-Forest in 8% and 2%; DragonNet had the strongest robustness curve. On the synthetic dataset, clean-data results diverged by metric: for ATE, R-Forest was best and outperformed all neural network models, with omnibus test statistic 174.25 and p = 8.6e-11, while BART and C-Forest were worst; for PEHE, C-Forest was best followed by BART, with omnibus test statistic 469.41 and p = 1.76e-10, C-Forest achieved the best PEHE in 80% of simulations and BART in 30%, while R-Forest and all neural network models did not achieve the best PEHE in any simulation and neural network models were worst in robustness; NEDnet and Dragonnet outperformed TARNet, with TARNet worst.
[context]
Experiments used IHDP, a semi-synthetic dataset with 747 units, 25 covariates, 608 control and 139 treated units, and 1000 realizations from the NPCI package, plus a synthetic dataset generated with a hidden confounder Z, mixture-Bernoulli treatment, mixture-Gaussian proxy covariates, and a logistic-sigmoid outcome. Models were implemented in Python 3.7 with Keras and used the hyperparameters specified for each model in the paper; no hardware, training time, or perturbation settings are reported. Evaluation was on clean data using ATE and PEHE, not under uniform noise, FGSM, OTCM, or other covariate perturbations, and no ablation of adversarial or Lipschitz terms was performed. Therefore, the paper supports rigorous baseline comparison and statistical reporting for HTE estimators but does not directly validate the proposed robustness claims or the full set of 11 baselines in the research plan.
