[method]
The paper contains a benchmark of HTE estimators that is directly relevant to the proposed baseline comparison because it evaluates several of the same estimator families: S-Learner, T-Learner, Causal Forest, Balancing Neural Network, and TARNet. It also discusses Counterfactual Regression and uses CFR as the regression function for plugin validation and counterfactual cross-validation, although it does not evaluate CFR as a standalone HTE estimator in the main comparison. The paper does not evaluate Random Forests as a standalone HTE estimator, DragonNet, DeRCFR, ESCFR, or any adversarial or Lipschitz-regularized model, so it provides baseline context rather than direct evidence for the proposed robustness components. In the experiments, estimators are trained on all observed features without access to the causal graph, except for an Oracle known-structure method and a structure-learning O-set method. The evaluation covers synthetic random SCMs, the IHDP semi-synthetic dataset, and real-world datasets, using PEHE/RMSE when ground truth is available, sign accuracy on synthetic data, tau-risk as a proxy when ground truth is unavailable, and average rank across SCMs.
[results]
On 10,000-sample synthetic SCMs, the paper reports average RMSE with standard deviations. Causal Forest: 9.31 ± 2.53 for mediator chain length 0, 7.47 ± 2.50 for chain length 1, 6.78 ± 2.48 for chain length 2, 7.77 ± 3.05 with confounding, 8.15 ± 2.46 without confounding, 8.10 ± 2.71 with 0 HTE parents, 8.13 ± 2.69 with 1 HTE parent, 8.37 ± 2.66 with 2 HTE parents, 7.93 ± 2.76 with 10 variables, 7.77 ± 2.71 with 20 variables, and 7.77 ± 2.71 with 30 variables. Balancing Neural Network: 4.74 ± 2.39, 4.39 ± 2.08, 4.47 ± 2.27, 4.88 ± 2.73, 4.31 ± 1.82, 4.47 ± 2.24, 4.58 ± 2.24, 4.57 ± 2.28, 5.01 ± 2.35, 4.33 ± 2.22, and 4.38 ± 2.17 in the same column order. TARNet: 7.86 ± 2.40, 8.08 ± 2.22, 7.62 ± 2.42, 7.13 ± 2.58, 8.40 ± 2.03, 7.83 ± 2.23, 7.93 ± 2.41, 7.86 ± 2.41, 8.18 ± 2.33, 7.90 ± 2.31, and 7.65 ± 2.38. S-Learner: 8.19 ± 1.85, 9.04 ± 1.95, 9.20 ± 1.97, 8.52 ± 2.06, 8.93 ± 1.88, 8.92 ± 1.92, 8.72 ± 1.98, 8.65 ± 1.99, 8.54 ± 2.00, 8.78 ± 1.94, and 8.89 ± 1.95. T-Learner: 6.36 ± 1.68, 6.99 ± 1.81, 7.08 ± 1.87, 6.50 ± 2.00, 6.97 ± 1.63, 6.96 ± 1.70, 6.71 ± 1.83, 6.66 ± 1.87, 6.54 ± 1.83, 6.85 ± 1.78, and 6.88 ± 1.80. The structure-learning O-set method had lower RMSE than most data-driven baselines, for example 3.02 ± 1.59 for mediator chain length 0 and 2.78 ± 1.30 without confounding, while the Oracle known-structure method had the lowest values, for example 2.08 ± 1.68 for mediator chain length 0 and 1.60 ± 1.00 without confounding. The paper reports that structure learning achieved the lowest average rank over most datasets, Causal Tree and Balancing Neural Network achieved lower average RMSE, and DR-Learner had high error and variance. No formal significance tests are reported; the reported ± values are standard deviations. On 1,000-sample synthetic data, no clear sample-size-dependent winner was found. For heuristic metrics, most metrics correlated positively with ground-truth rankings on synthetic data except plugin validation, with large variance and generally low average correlation; on IHDP, all metrics except plugin validation correlated positively, with tau-risk having the highest correlation. On real-world data, Causal Forest had the best tau-risk rank on League of Legends, XLearner had the best rank on Cannabis, and SLearner had the best rank on SHARPS.
[context]
The synthetic experiments used 11,000 random SCMs, with one dataset of 10,000 units and one dataset of 1,000 units per SCM. SCM parameters included 10, 20, or 30 variables; edge probability 0.1, 0.3, or 0.5; noise variance 0.2, 0.4, or 0.6; noise magnitude 0.1, 0.5, or 0.9; confounding true or false; mediator chain length 0, 1, or 2; 0, 1, or 2 HTE-inducing parents; and HTE from mediating parents true or false. The main data-driven estimators used all features and did not use the true graph. The Oracle method used the known optimal adjustment set with a T-Learner. Meta-learners used logistic regression and linear regression for propensity and outcome estimation on synthetic data, while random forest was used for outcome estimation on semi-synthetic and real-world data. IHDP had 747 units and 25 covariates, with 1,000 simulated outcomes. Real-world datasets included League of Legends with 1.2 million players, 437,000 matches, and 344 features; Cannabis with 194 Empath feature variables and bootstrap standard deviations; and SHARPS with 239 samples and 316 features, also using bootstrap standard deviations. The paper does not report hardware, training time, or detailed hyperparameters for BNN, TARNet, Causal Forest, or the meta-learners, and it does not test covariate perturbation, adversarial samples, Lipschitz regularization, or the proposed RHTE objective. Therefore, its results are relevant as clean-covariate baseline performance and metric behavior, but they do not directly establish the robustness gains claimed by the proposed adversarial and Lipschitz components.
