[method]
The paper's relevant method is ESCFR, a representation-based counterfactual regression model included in the proposed baseline set. It uses a shared covariate representation network and separate treated and control outcome heads, then adds a stochastic optimal transport objective to reduce treatment-group distribution shift. The transport cost is adjusted by a relaxed mass-preserving regularizer that softly downweights units outside the overlap region, and by a proximal factual outcome regularizer that incorporates estimated counterfactual outcome distances to account for unobserved confounders. This relates to the proposed robust HTE plan because ESCFR is one of the 11 representative HTE estimators used to determine whether robustness under covariate perturbation is due to adversarial and Lipschitz components rather than generic representation balancing or model capacity.
[results]
On IHDP and ACIC, ESCFR is reported to improve over most baselines on PEHE and AUUC, with the gains attributed to the relaxed mass-preserving and proximal factual outcome regularizers. The provided excerpt gives CFR-WASS out-of-sample PEHE of 3.207 on ACIC and states that ESCFR achieves significant improvement over most prevalent baselines, but exact ESCFR table values are not included. In the hyperparameter study, increasing the transport weight lambda from 0 to 1.5 lowers out-of-sample PEHE from 3.22 to about 2.85; larger entropic epsilon increases PEHE with oscillations; excessive PFOR weight or too low RMPR mass penalty degrades performance. No statistical significance tests are reported.
[context]
Experiments use semi-synthetic IHDP with 747 observations and 25 covariates and ACIC with 4,802 observations and 58 covariates. Neural models use a fully connected network with two 60-dimensional hidden layers, Adam optimizer, maximum 400 epochs, learning rate 1e-3, weight decay 1e-4, early stopping patience 30, validation every two epochs, and hyperparameter tuning. PEHE is the primary metric, AUUC guides model selection because counterfactuals are missing, and within-sample and out-of-sample results are reported. Hardware is not specified. This context matters for the proposed plan because it uses the same ACIC and IHDP benchmarks and includes ESCFR as a baseline, but the proposed plan adds uniform, FGSM, and OTCM covariate perturbations, adversarial loss, Lipschitz regularization, and a 3-layer 200/100-unit architecture, and lists ACIC with 82 covariates, so feature alignment and perturbation protocol would differ from the paper's non-perturbed OT/IPM setup.
