[method]
The paper describes a Gromov-Wasserstein information-bottleneck extension of counterfactual regression for heterogeneous treatment effect estimation. The approach uses an encoder to map covariates into a representation space and introduces a transport-plan variable to balance treatment and control groups through an optimal-transport-based regularization, while also bounding empirical kernelized mutual information. The optimization uses alternating optimization: with the encoder fixed, the lower-level transport-plan problem is solved using a conditional gradient method, and the transport plan is updated by projected gradient descent with an elementwise step size determined by line search. The procedure is repeated until the lower-level objective converges, after which model parameters are updated. This method is related to the proposed research method and research plan because it is a neural-network-based counterfactual regression variant that could serve as a baseline in the proposed comparison of 11 HTE estimators, and it addresses representation balancing for treatment effect estimation, though the supplied excerpt does not indicate that it explicitly handles covariate perturbation or Lipschitz robustness.
