[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Willmott, and Matsuura, 2005-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/581d0024eccf55493dd7d63554063a683bef6103)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 6469
[method]
The paper performs an analytical and illustrative assessment of two average-error metrics, MAE and RMSE. It defines MAE as the sum of absolute errors divided by n and RMSE as the square root of the sum of squared errors divided by n. It then constructs a hypothetical set of error magnitudes in which error-magnitude variance increases from zero in Case 1 to its maximum in Case 5, and it examines a set of MAE/RMSE pairs computed from differences among five mean-annual global precipitation fields. The evaluation setup is a metric-comparison exercise: for each error set or difference field, MAE and RMSE are calculated and their relationship is inspected, with error-magnitude variance treated as the explanatory factor. No causal inference algorithm, treatment-effect estimator, or HTE dataset is used.
[results]
The paper reports that RMSE increases steadily as error-magnitude variance increases from Case 1 to Case 5. It derives that RMSE is bounded below by MAE, attained when all absolute errors are equal, and bounded above by sqrt(n) times MAE, attained when all prediction error is concentrated in a single error of size n times MAE. In the precipitation-field example, RMSE is always larger than MAE, and the RMSE-MAE difference increases with MAE but not monotonically. The scatter between MAE and RMSE is attributed to differing error-magnitude variances because n and the covered area are constant across the compared difference fields. The paper does not report numerical MAE or RMSE values in the provided excerpt.
[context]
The hypothetical example uses n errors and five cases spanning zero to maximum error-magnitude variance. The precipitation example uses five mean-annual global precipitation fields, with each MAE/RMSE pair calculated from the same difference field and with constant n or grid area. The paper emphasizes that if n, area, or time domain differed, RMSE would also vary with sqrt(n) and with the variability of error magnitudes, so RMSE differences could not be attributed to average error alone. This is directly relevant to the proposed method because the proposed √PEHE metric is an RMSE-type measure of CATE estimation error. In the research plan, √PEHE is used as the primary outcome for within-sample and out-of-sample CATE accuracy under clean and perturbed covariates across datasets with different sample sizes and covariate dimensions. The paper's findings suggest that √PEHE may conflate average CATE error with the variance of CATE errors and with sample/domain size, so robustness conclusions under covariate perturbation should be supported by additional metrics such as MAE or an explicit error-magnitude variance measure, especially when comparing within-sample versus out-of-sample performance or across datasets.
[end related work 1]

