[method]
The paper evaluates a meta-learning based counterfactual inference method, MetaITE, for binary and multiple treatment settings. MetaITE is built on a base inference model and uses episodic training with local and global updates, together with an MMD discrepancy measure to reduce covariate distribution discrepancy across treatment groups. For the binary-treatment experiments most directly related to the proposed research method, the paper uses the Rooted Precision in Estimation of Heterogeneous Effect, or root PEHE, as a primary evaluation metric alongside mean absolute error on ATE. The metric is applied to out-of-sample test performance on Twins_bin and News_2, where the task is to estimate factual and counterfactual outcomes for two treatments. This aligns with the proposed method’s use of root PEHE as a standard CATE accuracy metric, but the paper does not report within-sample root PEHE and does not evaluate covariate perturbation, which are central to the broader research plan.
[results]
