[method]
The paper uses ground-truth PEHE, defined as the root mean squared error between predicted individual treatment effects and true individual effects, as the primary accuracy measure when effects are known. It applies this metric in a synthetic structural causal model benchmark by generating 11,000 SCMs with 10, 20, or 30 variables, edge probabilities of 0.1, 0.3, or 0.5, noise variance of 0.2, 0.4, or 0.6, noise magnitude of 0.1, 0.5, or 0.9, confounding on or off, mediator chain length 0, 1, or 2, 0, 1, or 2 heterogeneity-inducing parents, and heterogeneity from mediating parents on or off. For each SCM, datasets of 10,000 and 1,000 units are created, and CATE estimators including BART, causal trees, causal forests, S/T/X/DR learners, balancing neural networks, TARNet, a structure-learning O-set method, and an oracle known-structure T-learner are evaluated. This directly relates to the proposed method because the proposed research plan also uses rooted PEHE as the primary CATE accuracy metric, but the paper evaluates clean synthetic and semi-synthetic settings rather than covariate-perturbed, adversarial, or Lipschitz-regularized robustness settings.
[results]
In the 10,000-sample synthetic SCM evaluation, the oracle known-structure method achieved the lowest conditional average RMSE values, including 2.08 ± 1.68 for mediator chain length 0, 1.95 ± 1.91 for mediator chain length 1, and 2.46 ± 2.55 for mediator chain length 2. Among non-oracle methods, FSL(O-set) and causal tree had among the lowest RMSE values; FSL(O-set) was 3.02 ± 1.59, 3.14 ± 1.86, and 3.24 ± 2.21 for mediator chain lengths 0, 1, and 2, while causal tree was 3.74 ± 3.23, 3.36 ± 2.65, and 3.70 ± 2.99 for the same settings. Balancing neural network had RMSE values of 4.74 ± 2.39, 4.39 ± 2.08, and 4.47 ± 2.27 for those same mediator chain lengths. The paper reports that FSL(O-set) achieved the lowest average rank over most settings, causal tree and balancing neural network achieved lower average RMSE, and DR-learner had high error and variance. In the 1,000-sample ranking experiment, the paper did not find strong sample-size-dependent differences in relative method performance. In the IHDP semi-synthetic evaluation, heuristic metrics except plugin validation correlated positively with ground-truth ranking, with tau-risk having the highest correlation, supporting the use of ground-truth PEHE-style evaluation when true effects are available.
[context]
The synthetic experiments assume estimators do not know the causal graph and use all generated features, which mimics real-world use where invalid variables such as mediators or descendants of treatment may be present. The oracle method uses the known graph and an optimal adjustment set. Meta-learners use logistic and linear regression on synthetic data and random forests on semi-synthetic and real data. IHDP has 747 units with 6 continuous and 19 binary features, and 1,000 simulated outcomes are averaged. Real-world datasets lack ground truth, so the paper uses tau-risk rather than PEHE. No hardware, training time, or covariate perturbation protocol is reported, and the paper does not explicitly separate within-sample from out-of-sample PEHE or test clean versus perturbed covariates. Therefore, it provides a clean-data PEHE evaluation baseline and metric-reliability context for the proposed robust HTE plan, but not direct evidence for the proposed adversarial perturbation or Lipschitz-regularized robustness claims.
