- in_text_citation: [(Bottou et al., 2012-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b006aefa9c8be8c33138c4c5352f4d2d44378be9)
- venue: Journal of machine learning research
- citation count: 893
[method]
The paper uses a randomized reserve-multiplier experiment in a Bing ad placement engine to estimate the counterfactual effect of changing a scalar system parameter. Before determining the ad layout, a standard normal random variable is drawn and all mainline reserves are multiplied by a log-normal multiplier with mean rho and width sigma. The main treatment bucket used rho = 1 and sigma = 0.3, so 95% of multipliers fell in the range [0.52, 1.74]. Page views were randomly assigned to traffic buckets without regard to user identity to approximate an isolation assumption. The collected randomized data were then reweighted by the ratio of the target log-normal density to the collection density to estimate what would have been measured under other reserve-scaling parameters rho* while keeping sigma* = sigma. The evaluation estimated 95% confidence intervals for average mainline ads per page, average clicks per page, and average revenue per page as functions of rho*. This method is related to the proposed beta ablation because both vary a scalar parameter to isolate its contribution to downstream performance, and it is relevant to the overall robust HTE plan as a causal counterfactual evaluation of an intervention, although the paper studies ad reserves rather than Lipschitz regularization or covariate perturbation.
[results]
The experiment collected 22 million search result pages over five consecutive weeks. The counterfactual estimates showed that inner confidence intervals grow sharply when rho* leaves the range explored during data collection, and revenue estimates had larger variance because a few competitive queries command high prices. A second equal-size bucket with mainline reserves reduced by about 18% was used for validation; the measured metrics in that bucket matched the counterfactual estimates with high accuracy, although the text does not provide numerical differences. A control bucket running the unmodified system showed that randomization caused a small but statistically significant increase in the number of mainline ads per page, while differences in click yield and average revenue were not statistically significant. No HTE, PEHE, or Lipschitz-regularization outcomes are reported.
[context]
The setting was Bing search result pages, with 22 million page views collected over five weeks. The key constraint was the isolation assumption: the counterfactual question ignored user and advertiser reactions, so page views were randomized independently of user identity. The randomization parameters were rho = 1 and sigma = 0.3, producing log-normal multipliers with mean 1 and a 95% range of [0.52, 1.74]. Validation used an equal-size bucket with reserves reduced by about 18% and a control bucket without randomization. The metrics were average mainline ads per page, average clicks per page, and average revenue per page, with 95% confidence intervals and inner confidence intervals. No hardware, software, or computational resource details are reported. The method is not a direct ablation of a machine-learning regularization term; its transferable context is the use of randomized parameter perturbation, reweighting, and held-out experimental validation to estimate the causal effect of changing a system parameter.
[end related work 1]

