aper/7cec4f49d1e1c987a7d1e2b4e213cc7c05ea8788), which shows unweighted KRR with lambda ||f||_H^2 is minimax-optimal up to logarithmic factors under B-bounded likelihood ratios and that regularized KRR over a larger effective Hilbert ball can outperform constrained kernel regression under covariate shift, supporting norm-penalized RKHS models under distribution shift but not adversarial perturbation. Older empirical support includes [(Evgeniou et al., 2005-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0af75728bec67f698a8c619645165de13780c2fa), whose multi-task kernel machines with gamma-weighted squared RKHS norm reduce error by 1.14, 1.56, and 2.07 percentage points at 20 points per task for 50, 100, and 200 tasks, and [(Wang, and Ruppert, 2013-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/368b2d28d08dc158eb87d321be88632bfa1acb49), whose thin-plate spline RKHS roughness regularizer reduces California RMSPE from 0.9450 to 0.6148 over 1000 splits. Weaker topical support comes from [(Grünewälder et al., 2012-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba31e44c8e5df7f899e4e497c56e17dd5476d85a) and [(Grünewälder et al., 2012-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6ececdbff150de286c44974d486254302fc15800), which use regularized vector-valued RKHS embeddings for MDP value estimation and conditional mean embeddings, and from [(Micchelli, and Pontil, unknown_d)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d37ad77c8da2e5c3b767b2ab452694c75ba65e3e), which is only a vector-valued RKHS regularization outline without extractable results.",
  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is theoretical: [(Ma et al., 2022-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7cec4f49d1e1c987a7d1e2b4e213cc7c05ea8788) shows constrained kernel regression over the smallest Hilbert ball containing the true function can be sub-optimal under covariate shift, implying that a fixed RKHS-norm ball or overly tight norm constraint may not be the right robustness device; the paper favors regularized KRR over a larger effective ball, so the proposed plan should avoid treating the RKHS norm as a hard Lipschitz certificate without validation. The next strongest empirical contradiction is [(Evgeniou et al., 2005-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0af75728bec67f698a8c619645165de13780c2fa), where RKHS-norm multi-task regularization can hurt if the coupling or regularization parameter is misspecified: with 200 tasks and 20 points per task the error has a flat minimum between lambda 0.4 and 0.6, while with 10 tasks only lambda 0.7 to 1 beats single-task, and one SVM for all tasks has 38 to 42 percent error, showing that a fixed alpha=beta=1 or unvalidated beta may degrade performance. [(Wang et al., 2023-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/095825ad35409b853622ab7665663797058fc3e6) also contradicts a blanket claim that RKHS-norm balancing is uniformly best, because CFB does not achieve the smallest average MSE in Setting 2, where REG is better, although CFB has smallest standard errors in all settings. A weaker but important limitation is that [(Bietti et al., 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0c7398fce2ba733614997904f30654e4628cbbe7) provides no quantitative evidence that RKHS-norm lower-bound penalties improve adversarial accuracy or robustness, so its methodological relevance does not establish that the proposed RKHS term will reduce sqrt PEHE under uniform, FGSM, or OTCM perturbations. Similarly, [(Jiang et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/950e1ea064b835a9ea6210f86b75888601f03fe9) demonstrates kernel or GP smoothness for HTE but does not test covariate perturbation, so it cannot support the adversarial robustness claim. Older works such as [(Grünewälder et al., 2012-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba31e44c8e5df7f899e4e497c56e17dd5476d85a), [(Grünewälder et al., 2012-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6ececdbff150de286c44974d486254302fc15800), [(Wang, and Ruppert, 2013-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/368b2d28d08dc158eb87d321be88632bfa1acb49), and [(Micchelli, and Pontil, unknown_d)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d37ad77c8da2e5c3b767b2ab452694c75ba65e3e) are not causal or adversarial, and the latter provides no extractable quantitative outcome, so they offer only weak or indirect support and cannot validate the proposed robust HTE use.",
  "suggested_action": "Most important, define the RKHS concretely for the 3-layer fully connected causal model: specify a kernel on covariates, for example a Gaussian or RBF kernel with bandwidth selected by validation, and either train a kernel ridge or GP surrogate for the CATE or use the neural tangent kernel of the 200-unit representation and 100-unit head to compute an explicit RKHS norm penalty; do not assume the deep network parameters define the RKHS norm. Second, validate that the RKHS penalty actually controls Lipschitz behavior under the planned perturbations by deriving or empirically measuring the bound between adversarial loss and factual loss, and by reporting sensitivity to beta over the planned grid 0, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1, 1e2, and 1e4 rather than fixing alpha=beta=1, using cross-validation or leave-one-out as in [(Wang et al., 2023-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/095825ad35409b853622ab7665663797058fc3e6) and [(Ma et al., 2022-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7cec4f49d1e1c987a7d1e2b4e213cc7c05ea8788). Third, combine and ablate the RKHS term with orthonormality or spectral-norm regularization and IPM, following the spectral-norm and lower-bound smoothness penalties in [(Bietti et al., 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0c7398fce2ba733614997904f30654e4628cbbe7), and report separate ablations for spherical, uniform U(-0.1,0.1)^dim(x), FGSM, and OTCM perturbations with sqrt PEHE, within-sample and out-of-sample results, and statistical significance. Fourth, incorporate causal balancing insights from [(Wang et al., 2023-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/095825ad35409b853622ab7665663797058fc3e6) by treating the RKHS norm as a balancing criterion over treatment and control representations and by comparing against REG, FCBPS, and NW-style baselines where appropriate. Fifth, use the multi-task kernel HTE evidence from [(Jiang et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/950e1ea064b835a9ea6210f86b75888601f03fe9)