[method]
The closest related method in the paper is CB-IV and its confounder-balancing baselines, especially Rep, CFR, and DRCFR. CB-IV is a three-stage deep representation-learning instrumental-variable regression method: (i) treatment regression estimates the treatment distribution or treatment value from instruments Z and observed covariates X; (ii) confounder balancing learns a representation C = f_theta(X) to reduce observed-confounder imbalance; and (iii) outcome regression predicts potential outcomes or the outcome using the estimated treatment and the balanced representation. For binary treatment, the paper uses two separate outcome heads, h0(C) and h1(C), which is structurally similar to the proposed 200-unit representation function plus 100-unit treatment-effect prediction function. The confounder-balancing baselines are also related because they learn representations for treatment-effect estimation and are conceptually connected to the proposed RHTE framework’s use of representation learning and distribution balancing. However, the paper’s method is not directly about covariate perturbation robustness: it does not use adversarial perturbation loss, Lipschitz or RKHS regularization, or PEHE under perturbed covariates.
[results]
On synthetic binary-treatment datasets with 10,000 units and 10 replications, CB-IV improved over traditional IV methods and generally outperformed confounder-balancing methods, although when observed confounders were high-dimensional relative to low-dimensional instruments its behavior approached CFR. In data-size experiments on Syn-2-4-4 with 500, 1000, 5000, and 10000 samples, CB-IV had low bias but high variance at small sample sizes; above 5000 samples its upper error bound was below the lower error bounds of the baselines. On real-world IHDP and Twins with generated IVs, CB-IV was best overall: without unmeasured confounders it beat DRCFR, CFR, and Rep, while IV-only methods were weakest; with unmeasured confounders confounder-balancing baselines degraded substantially, whereas CB-IV and IV methods were less affected, though IHDP’s 471-sample training split gave CB-IV small bias but large variance. In the reported mixed-variable ATE table with 10,000 units and 10 replications, CB-IV had bias 0.493 (0.261), 0.528 (0.099), 0.114 (0.071), and 0.017 (0.046) across the four settings, while DFL was 0.843 (0.002) in all settings, Rep was 0.164 (0.009), 0.164 (0.009), 0.179 (0.019), and 0.164 (0.009), CFR was 0.159 (0.018), 0.159 (0.018), 0.178 (0.023), and 0.159 (0.018), and DRCFR was 0.137 (0.035), 0.137 (0.035), 0.142 (0.052), and 0.137 (0.035).
[context]
Experiments used synthetic binary and continuous demand datasets, semi-synthetic IHDP with 747 units, 139 treated units, 608 control units, 2-dimensional random IVs, and 6 confounders, and Twins with 5,271 same-sex twin records and 5-dimensional random IVs. Real-world experiments used 100 IHDP realizations and 10 Twins realizations with 63/27/10 train/validation/test splits. Mixed-variable experiments used 10,000 units and 10 replications across four input settings: stage-1 Z with stage-2 X, X-only, mixed Z and X in both stages, and mixed Z and X in stage 1 with X in stage 2. The paper reports ATE bias and standard deviation, not PEHE under covariate perturbation, and does not report the proposed 3-layer 200/100-unit ReLU/batch-normalization architecture, alpha = beta = 1, batch size 64, or learning rate 0.01. It notes that CB-IV-L requires at least about 5,000 samples for reliable latent-variable reconstruction and that CB-IV generally needs larger samples for variance convergence.
[end related work 1]

Processing method: Context: The neural implementation must be specified for reproducibility. Method: We will implement our methods using a 3-layer fully-connected architecture with 200 units for the representation function and 100 units for the treatment effect prediction function. We will use ReLU activations, batch normalization, and set hyperparameters α = β = 1, with batch size 64 and learning rate 0.01. Synthesis: These implementation details define the concrete model and training setup used in the experiments.
