 increasing the TRADES robust regularization parameter lambda increases perturbation stability and can improve robust accuracy, for example from 54.18% to 56.43% for width-10 WideResNet-34 on CIFAR10 when lambda is raised from 6 to 18, with stability increasing from 67.25% to 72.11%. This supports the plan's hypothesis that stronger smoothness or Lipschitz-related regularization can improve worst-case performance, but it also implies that regularization strength must be tuned. Additional support comes from [(Jakubovitz, and Giryes, 2018-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba6bc847a6ece8448ff492d0639a93d736427903), where post-processing Jacobian regularization with lambda = 0.1 on MNIST and lambda = 0.5 on CIFAR-10 produced smaller DeepFool perturbation norms and higher FGSM perturbed accuracy than several baselines, supporting norm-based smoothness penalties. Weaker but conceptually relevant support comes from [(Cissé et al., 2017-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/013efe3ff541e518c51f08d1b62a62e0c57c0b14) and [(Anil et al., 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f0ded4902d7f9c111e50047f8c9494effb7282d1), which motivate Parseval, orthonormal, or spectral-norm constraints for Lipschitz control, although the supplied excerpts do not provide quantitative causal or robustness outcomes. Finally, [(Krishnan et al., 2020-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c41abf3e96ed10ff175652d4f26f49179f109ea4) supports the mechanism by showing that tighter Lipschitz bounds reduce perturbation sensitivity, but its evidence is weaker because it is a graph-based MNIST classifier and reports mainly qualitative trends.",
  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is [(Krishnan et al., 2020-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c41abf3e96ed10ff175652d4f26f49179f109ea4), because it directly shows that increasing the Lipschitz bound alpha increases testing accuracy and confidence while decreasing testing loss, and that perturbation sensitivity increases with alpha. The imposed Lipschitz constraint is active only for alpha < 175 and inactive for alpha >= 175, and increasing the loss margin decreases the classifier Lipschitz constant, testing accuracy, and confidence. This contradicts a fixed Lipschitz regularization strength in the plan and shows that smoothness control can degrade factual performance if it is too tight. The next strongest contradiction is [(Wu et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/99931b7b794f7d98c7b3f3eee862129133d123d7), which finds that perturbation stability decreases monotonically as network width increases and that robust accuracy does not always improve with width; the best TRADES lambda shifts from 9 for width-1 to 12 for width-5 to 18 for width-10 on CIFAR10. This contradicts using a single fixed alpha = beta = 1 across model capacities and suggests that the plan's ablation over beta must be tied to validation under perturbed PEHE rather than treated as a universal default. A further contradiction for the theoretical formulation is [(Hein, and Andriushchenko, 2017-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/255d2c2af6d7abbbebfc03dab51cd8574ad3558e), which reports that local Lipschitz or cross-Lipschitz bounds can be up to 8 times smaller than global Lipschitz bounds, indicating that a global Lipschitz constant in the proposed adversarial-to-factual loss bound may be too coarse or vacuous for meaningful robust CATE guarantees. [(Weng et al., 2018-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/72de5578808c87c5bab305e46854fdf0b0b2ddab) reinforces this by showing that robustness certification depends on estimating an extreme local Lipschitz constant, not merely a global one. [(Jakubovitz, and Giryes, 2018-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba6bc847a6ece8448ff492d0639a93d736427903) also contradicts a norm-agnostic regularization design: its l2-oriented Jacobian penalty was inferior to Input Gradient regularization on MNIST under JSMA, an l0-style attack, showing that the regularization norm must match the perturbation norm. Weaker contradictions or limitations include [(Anil et al., 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f0ded4902d7f9c111e50047f8c9494effb7282d1), where strict 1-Lipschitz architectures may constrain representational capacity, and [(Cissé et al., 2017-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/013efe3ff541e518c51f08d1b62a62e0c57c0b14), whose supplied excerpt lacks quantitative outcomes. More broadly, none of these works directly evaluates CATE, IPM balancing, treatment-control balance, or sqrtPEHE, so their support for the causal plan is indirect.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to make the theoretical bound local, cross-Lipschitz, or margin-aware rather than relying only on a global Lipschitz constant, and to estimate the relevant Lipschitz constant empirically for the CATE model using gradient sampling, reverse Weibull fitting, or CLEVER-style diagnostics, because [(Hein, and Andriushchenko, 2017-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/255d2c2af6d7abbbebfc03dab51cd8574ad3558e) and [(Weng et al., 2018-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/72de5578808c87c5bab305e46854fdf0b0b2ddab) show that global bounds can be too coarse and local estimation is feasible. Second, align the regularization with the perturbation norm: use l2 spectral, Parseval, or Jacobian penalties for the spherical epsilon-ball, but add linf or l0-aware adversarial training or norm-specific penalties for FGSM and OTCM, following the norm-mismatch evidence in [(Jakubovitz, and Giryes, 2018-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba6bc847a6ece8448ff492d0639a93d736427903). Third, tune beta by validation under perturbed sqrtPEHE and model capacity rather than fixing alpha = beta = 1; report whether the Lipschitz constraint is active and use the planned beta grid [0, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1, 1e2, 1e4] with multiple seeds and significance tests, following the capacity-dependent lambda results in [(Wu et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/99931b7b794f7d98c7b3f3eee862129133d123d7) and the active-constraint tradeoff in [(Krishnan et al., 2020-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c41abf3e96ed10ff175652d4f26f49179f109ea4). Fourth, consider a two-stage or joint optimization strategy: first train the causal representation with weighted empirical loss and IPM, then apply post-processing Lipschitz regularization or TRADES-like robust regularization, because [(Jakubovitz, and Giryes, 2018-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba6bc847a6ece8448ff492d0639a93d736427903) found post-processing can preserve clean accuracy and [(Wu et al., 2020-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/99931b7b794f7d98c7b3f3eee862129133d123d7) found robust regularization improves perturbation stability. Fifth, implement orthonormal or Parseval constraints with power iteration, Björck orthonormalization, or spectral-norm control, and include an RKHS cross-Lipschitz penalty where appropriate, while monitoring clean PEHE and capacity, following [(Cissé et al., 2017-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/013efe3ff541e518c51f08d1b62a62e0c57c0b14), [(Anil et al., 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f0ded4902d7f9c111e50047f8c9494effb7282d1), [(Farnia et al., 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3a606480406886742572a956e221e986c65d94c1), and [(Hein, and Andriushchenko, 2017-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/255d2c2af6d7abbbebfc03dab51cd8574ad3558e). Finally, add causal-specific evaluation details: within-sample and out-of-sample sqrtPEHE under uniform, FGSM, and OTCM perturbations, comparisons to the 11 planned baselines, hardware, random seeds, statistical significance, and reported local Lipschitz estimates or certified perturbation radii where possible.",
