  "support": "The strongest support is that root PEHE is already used as a primary CATE accuracy metric in recent HTE evaluation: [(Zhou et al., 2022-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8a399313f74f087f543b701207804a2b44392500) reports root PEHE alongside MAE on ATE for out-of-sample Twins_bin and News_2, with 80/20 splits, 10 repeated runs, and imbalanced treatment sizes (Twins_bin 4594 vs 80; News_2 1634 vs 160), directly supporting the plan's use of √PEHE for out-of-sample CATE accuracy. A second strong support is [(Tran et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d39946a79a0fb6647c14455b99144a51cc7cef64) which uses ground-truth PEHE as the primary accuracy measure in synthetic SCMs with 10,000 and 1,000 units, 10-30 variables, and many CATE estimators, reporting RMSE values such as oracle 2.08 ± 1.68, FSL(O-set) 3.02 ± 1.59, causal tree 3.74 ± 3.23, and BNN 4.74 ± 2.39 for mediator chain length 0, which supports √PEHE as a clean-data benchmark metric when true effects are available. A third support is [(Zhao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5af8834324e7ed10a6515657f07841131d9fdbdd), whose protocol reports individual-level and group-level accuracy on both training and test sets with 100 repeated evaluations, conceptually supporting the plan's within-sample and out-of-sample reporting, although it does not explicitly name √PEHE. Weaker support comes from [(Han, and Wu, 2022-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fd37c98b39618f1aa8c62f452cb884a54eeed650), which evaluates ACIC semi-synthetic datasets with average test MSE over 50 replications, aligning with the plan's ACIC use but not √PEHE or perturbation, and from [(Ali et al., 2024-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/65277665b1fd4f25df17541b380d30239212e0cd), which uses PEHE and RMSE for synthetic spatiotemporal ground truth but not standard tabular within/out-of-sample CATE. Finally, [(Willmott, and Matsuura, 2005-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/581d0024eccf55493dd7d63554063a683bef6103) supports treating √PEHE as an RMSE-type metric with known bounds, but only insofar as it clarifies what RMSE measures.",
  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is [(Willmott, and Matsuura, 2005-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/581d0024eccf55493dd7d63554063a683bef6103), which shows that RMSE increases with error-magnitude variance, is bounded below by MAE and above by sqrt(n) times MAE, and can be affected by n, area, or time domain; because the plan compares √PEHE across ACIC 4,802/82, IHDP 747/25, UTK-sim 1,000/2,710, TC-sim 3,240/3,071, and within/out-of-sample, √PEHE alone may conflate average CATE error, error variance, and sample/domain scale, so robustness conclusions under perturbation could be misleading if only √PEHE is reported. Next, [(Tran et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d39946a79a0fb6647c14455b99144a51cc7cef64) uses ground-truth PEHE but only clean synthetic/semi-synthetic settings, does not separate within-sample from out-of-sample PEHE, and does not test covariate perturbation, so it does not validate √PEHE as a primary robustness metric under the plan's uniform, FGSM, or OTCM perturbations. [(Zhou et al., 2022-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8a399313f74f087f543b701207804a2b44392500) reports root PEHE only out-of-sample and does not report within-sample PEHE or perturbed covariates, leaving the plan's within-sample/out-of-sample contrast unsupported. [(Han, and Wu, 2022-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fd37c98b39618f1aa8c62f452cb884a54eeed650) uses average test MSE rather than √PEHE, provides no quantitative results in the excerpt, and does not test within-sample or perturbed covariates, so it offers limited direct support for the proposed metric choice. [(Zhao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5af8834324e7ed10a6515657f07841131d9fdbdd) reports train/test individual/group accuracy but does not explicitly use √PEHE and does not test covariate perturbation, so it supports evaluation structure but not the specific metric under adversarial perturbations. The weakest contradiction is [(Ali et al., 2024-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/65277665b1fd4f25df17541b380d30239212e0cd), which uses a customized PEHE in a spatiotemporal synthetic setting without a train/test split and without standard tabular CATE baselines, so it does not support the plan's standard within/out-of-sample √PEHE protocol.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to supplement √PEHE with MAE and an explicit error-magnitude variance or quantile metric, following [(Willmott, and Matsuura, 2005-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/581d0024eccf55493dd7d63554063a683bef6103) and [(Zhou et al., 2022-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8a399313f74f087f543b701207804a2b44392500), which pair root PEHE with MAE/ATE; report these alongside √PEHE for clean, uniform, FGSM, and OTCM perturbations, and for within-sample/out-of-sample, to prevent RMSE-type sensitivity to outliers and error variance from masking robustness. Second, define and report within-sample and out-of-sample √PEHE with explicit train/test splits, sample sizes, and repeated runs, using the repeated-evaluation style of [(Zhao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5af8834324e7ed10a6515657f07841131d9fdbdd) with 100 reps and [(Han, and Wu, 2022-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fd37c98b39618f1aa8c62f452cb884a54eeed650) with 50 reps, with mean and standard deviation and significance tests where possible. Third, align ACIC and IHDP evaluation with established protocols: use ACIC 43 high-heterogeneity datasets, 2000 training/1000 averaging/1802 test, and 50 replications as in [(Han, and Wu, 2022-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fd37c98b39618f1aa8c62f452cb884a54eeed650), and IHDP 747 units/25 covariates with 1,000 simulated outcomes or de-randomized settings as in [(Tran et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d39946a79a0fb6647c14455b99144a51cc7cef64) and [(Zhao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5af8834324e7ed10a6515657f07841131d9fdbdd), reporting √PEHE and RMSE/MAE. Fourth, for synthetic datasets where ground truth is available, use ground-truth √PEHE as in [(Tran et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d39946a79a0fb6647c14455b99144a51cc7cef64) and [(Ali et al., 2024-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/65277665b1fd4f25df17541b380d30239212e0cd), but also report RMSE and, if no ground truth is available, consider tau-risk or validation metrics as in [(Tran et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d39946a79a0fb6647c14455b99144a51cc7cef64). Fifth, for multimodal UTK-sim and TC-sim, specify how √PEHE is computed across 2,710 and 3,071 covariates, whether per-unit or aggregated, and include RMSE or MAE to account for high-dimensional error variance; if spatiotemporal or image components are used, follow [(Ali et al., 2024-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/65277665b1fd4f25df17541b380d30239212e0cd) by defining PEHE against synthetic ground truth and reporting no-split limitations. The least important action is to document implementation details such as hardware, random seeds, treatment imbalance, and perturbation norms to improve reproducibility, as many related works omit these details.",
