  "support": "Read as an iterative line, the strongest support for the proposed beta ablation is [(Johansson et al., 2016-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/759b00cf35b397eab468935b5d90d04e9ed25549), which ablates the imbalance penalty alpha in a BNN-2-2 counterfactual network on IHDP and a News simulation (n=5000, 3477-word vocabulary, 50 LDA topics, C=50, kappa=10, 50 realizations) and reports ITE RMSE, ATE absolute error, and PEHE; its alpha sensitivity analysis shows a performance valley near alpha=1 and no loss for smaller alpha, directly supporting the idea that a regularization-strength ablation can identify whether a penalty improves standard HTE estimation and where moderate values may be optimal. Next, [(Shi et al., 2019-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a278c07c8bd2921e59dd862cd91a0540dd340030) supports isolating a regularization contribution in Dragonnet/TARNet-style HTE models on IHDP and ACIC 2018 by comparing targeted regularization against baselines and reporting mean absolute error; it shows targeted regularization can improve over TARNET and that using all data can help, which supports the plan's focus on regularization ablation in neural HTE models, although it does not sweep beta or test covariate perturbation. [(Nie, and Wager, 2017-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/30eef96540a101e076d94e02651cb5c197059409) supports the RKHS regularization component by evaluating a kernel ridge regression R-learner with a Gaussian-kernel RKHS regularizer, selecting kernel width and ridge parameter by 5-fold cross-validation over 10^k with k from -3 to 3 in 0.5 increments, and evaluating test-set MSE for CATE; this supports using a regularization-strength grid and held-out test evaluation, though it does not address Lipschitz control or perturbation. Finally, [(Bottou et al., 2012-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b006aefa9c8be8c33138c4c5352f4d2d44378be9) supports the broader counterfactual parameter-perturbation logic: in a Bing ad placement experiment with 22 million page views over five weeks, it varied a scalar reserve multiplier with log-normal rho=1 and sigma=0.3 (95% range [0.52, 1.74]), reweighted data to estimate counterfactual rho* values, reported 95% confidence intervals for ads, clicks, and revenue per page, and validated with an equal-size bucket reducing reserves by about 18%; this supports varying a scalar parameter and validating counterfactual estimates, but it is not an HTE or Lipschitz ablation.",
  "contradictions": "No cited work directly contradicts a Lipschitz beta ablation, but the strongest limiting evidence is that [(Shi et al., 2019-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a278c07c8bd2921e59dd862cd91a0540dd340030) reports targeted regularization helps only about half the time on ACIC 2018, that TMLE hurts more than it helps on average, and that improvements are substantial when they occur while degradation is mild when they do not; this contradicts any assumption that adding a Lipschitz regularization term will uniformly improve standard HTE performance or robustness without dataset-specific tradeoffs. Next, [(Johansson et al., 2016-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/759b00cf35b397eab468935b5d90d04e9ed25549) shows that the balancing penalty alpha has a non-monotonic effect, with a valley near alpha=1 and no loss for smaller alpha, suggesting that high regularization strengths may not be beneficial; the proposed beta grid extends to 1e2 and 1e4, which may over-regularize a 3-layer 200/100 ReLU network with batch normalization unless the Lipschitz term is explicitly scaled to the loss. [(Bottou et al., 2012-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b006aefa9c8be8c33138c4c5352f4d2d44378be9) also contradicts overconfident extrapolation: its counterfactual confidence intervals grow sharply when rho* leaves the explored collection range, and revenue estimates had larger variance due to high-price queries, so a beta sweep alone should not be treated as reliable evidence of robustness beyond the tested beta and perturbation ranges. Finally, [(Nie, and Wager, 2017-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/30eef96540a101e076d94e02651cb5c197059409) shows that ridge regularization benefits depend on setup, with R-learner performing well in confounded Setups A and C but not uniformly best, and it provides no evidence that a fixed beta=1 or a wide beta range improves robustness under covariate perturbation.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to make the beta ablation quantitative and uncertainty-aware: for each beta in [0, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1, 1e2, 1e4], report sqrt PEHE on clean and perturbed test sets (uniform U(-0.1,0.1)^dim(x), FGSM, OTCM), standard HTE baselines (TARNet, DragonNet, CFR, BNN), multiple random seeds, confidence intervals or standard errors, and statistical significance tests, following the PEHE/ITE RMSE evaluation style of [(Johansson et al., 2016-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/759b00cf35b397eab468935b5d90d04e9ed25549) and the dataset-level improvement/degradation reporting of [(Shi et al., 2019-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a278c07c8bd2921e59dd862cd91a0540dd340030). Second, calibrate the beta range and selection protocol: normalize the Lipschitz penalty by the empirical loss scale, report empirical Lipschitz constants or spectral/RKHS norms, and use cross-validation or a pre-registered grid search similar to [(Nie, and Wager, 2017-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/30eef96540a101e076d94e02651cb5c197059409), while also ablating the IPM alpha separately and jointly to avoid conflating distribution balancing with Lipschitz control. Third, add a validation design inspired by [(Bottou et al., 2012-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b006aefa9c8be8c33138c4c5352f4d2d44378be9): use held-out perturbation buckets or randomized perturbation magnitudes to validate that beta-induced robustness estimates are not extrapolations, report inner confidence intervals or uncertainty, and avoid claiming robustness outside the tested beta and perturbation norms. Fourth, explicitly connect beta to the theoretical Lipschitz bound by measuring orthonormality/RKHS constraints, worst-case adversarial loss, and the effect of batch normalization and ReLU layers on the realized Lipschitz constant. Finally, report reproducibility details missing from the related work and plan, including hardware, software, seeds, covariate normalization for high-dimensional UTK-sim and TC-sim, batch size 64, learning rate 0.01, architecture 200/100, and perturbation norm definitions.",
  "soundness_score": 6
}
